{{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     post/article — the default reading surface.
     Ported from NS-Design-System/templates/blog-post.html.
     ------------------------------------------------------------------------
     Three things here are load-bearing and easy to remove by accident:

     · #post-body is on the PROSE div, not on <article>. It is what the
       reading-progress bar measures and what toc.js reads headings from.
       Move it up a level and both silently stop working.
     · data-toc-from lets toc.js build the outline from the article's own
       h2/h3 and add ids to any heading missing one. With JS off the rail is
       an empty labelled nav, which is why it carries a title.
     · .ns-prose is the 17px reading scale, and .gh-content is Ghost's — the
       theme has 34 rules scoped to it that restate Ghost's own cards.min.css
       in NSDS's vocabulary. Drop the class and those rules stop matching,
       Ghost's white bordered card CSS wins on the navy surface, and prose
       loses its list markers. Both classes, always.
     ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}
<span class="ns-lprogress ns-lprogress--article" data-target="#post-body"
      role="progressbar" aria-label="{{t "Reading progress"}}"
      aria-valuenow="0" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100"></span>

<header class="ns-posthead ns-posthead--center">
    <div class="ns-posthead__inner">
        {{#primary_tag}}<a class="ns-tag ns-posthead__cat" href="{{url}}">{{name}}</a>{{/primary_tag}}
        <h1 class="ns-posthead__title">{{title}}</h1>
        {{#if custom_excerpt}}<p class="ns-posthead__standfirst">{{custom_excerpt}}</p>{{/if}}

        <div class="ns-postmeta">
            {{#primary_author}}
                <span>
                    {{#if profile_image}}<img class="ns-postmeta__avatar" src="{{img_url profile_image size="xs"}}" alt="">{{/if}}
                    <a class="ns-postmeta__author" href="{{url}}">{{name}}</a>
                </span>
            {{/primary_author}}
            <span><time datetime="{{date format="YYYY-MM-DD"}}">{{date format="D MMMM YYYY"}}</time></span>
            <span>{{reading_time}}</span>
        </div>

        {{#if feature_image}}
            <img class="ns-posthead__cover" src="{{img_url feature_image size="xl"}}"
                 srcset="{{img_url feature_image size="l"}} 1000w, {{img_url feature_image size="xl"}} 2000w"
                 sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 1200px, 100vw"
                 alt="{{#if feature_image_alt}}{{feature_image_alt}}{{/if}}">
            {{#if feature_image_caption}}<span class="ns-posthead__credit">{{feature_image_caption}}</span>{{/if}}
        {{/if}}
    </div>
</header>

{{!-- ── THE SIDEBAR, AND THE ONE TAG THAT TURNS IT OFF ──────────────────────
     #hide-sidebar is a Ghost INTERNAL tag: invisible in the public tag list,
     absent from /tag/ routes, and therefore free to be used as a switch. Add
     it to a post in Admin and that post renders full width; remove it and the
     sidebar comes back. No theme setting, no custom template to pick, nothing
     to remember per post except a tag you can see in the editor.

     WHY A TAG AND NOT A THEME SETTING. A setting is site-wide, and this is a
     per-post decision — a wide photo essay and a reference article want
     different layouts on the same day. Ghost's own per-post lever is the
     internal tag, so that is the lever.

     THE GRID CLASS IS UPSTREAM'S. NSDS already ships both shapes:

       .ns-post--sidebar   prose + an 18rem column   (the default here)
       .ns-post--wide      prose alone               (#hide-sidebar)

     so neither branch invents a layout, and the two cannot drift. Note the
     bare `.ns-post` — three columns, with a share rail — is deliberately NOT
     used: this theme has no share rail, and the empty 3.5rem gutter it
     reserves pushed the prose measurably off-centre.

     ── ONE SIDE COLUMN, ON THE RIGHT, AND THE TOC IS IN IT ──────────────────
     This used to be THREE columns: a TOC rail on the left, prose, then the
     meta column on the right. Two rails around one 62ch article is a reading
     page with more furniture than text — the prose was pinched between them,
     and the two rails competed for the same glance because both are "things
     about this page".

     Now everything that is ABOUT the article lives in one right-hand column,
     outline first: partials/post/sidebar.hbs opens with the TOC and continues
     into the meta, topics and the ask. One column, one place to look, and the
     prose gets the whole of the rest.

     #hide-sidebar therefore removes the outline too, which is correct — it is
     one column now, and "hide the sidebar" means hide it. --}}
{{#has tag="#hide-sidebar"}}
    <div class="ns-post ns-post--wide">
        <article class="ns-post__body">
            <div class="gh-content ns-prose" id="post-body">{{content}}</div>
            {{!-- INSIDE the body column, not after the grid: comments are a
                 continuation of the reading column and must share its
                 measure. Dropped below the grid they would run the full page
                 width, which on a wide screen is a 140-character line — the
                 one thing .ns-prose exists to prevent. --}}
            {{> "comments"}}
        </article>
    </div>
{{else}}
    <div class="ns-post ns-post--sidebar">
        <article class="ns-post__body">
            <div class="gh-content ns-prose" id="post-body">{{content}}</div>
            {{> "comments"}}
        </article>

        {{> "post/sidebar"}}
    </div>
{{/has}}
